The '''''Second Mahik War''''' was fought from 1549 to 1558 between the [[Sinchew Colony]] and the [[Salowi]] and [[Chidoha]] peoples of the colony's western frontier. It ended in the defeat of both peoples and the annexation of their lands by the colony.

The war was fought without the authorisation of the [[Kingdom of Zong]]. No forces were sent from the mainland. The colony engaged the [[Sea Wolves]], Sanese mercenaries operating under no sovereign, and obtained 25,000 warriors from its ally the [[Hachinawa|Hahkina state]] in exchange for pork and iron. The [[Societal Assembly]] of Zong was informed of the annexation after the fact and raised no objection to it, and the governor of the colony was honoured for the extension of its territory.

Contemporary accounts did not describe the conflict as a war. The term used in the period, and retained in some later writing, is the 'Secret Sinchew War'. Later scholarship has treated it as a war without qualification, on the evidence of the destruction and displacement and the scale of the enslavement. The conflict is placed in sequence with the [[First Mahik War]] of 1538 to 1542 and both are treated as wars against the peoples of the region. Together with the earlier conflict it forms part of the [[First Lurandian Native Wars]], the wider series of campaigns fought in the Zong colonies between 1538 and the 1560s.

== Background ==

=== Land grant system ===

The lands acquired by the colony in the First Mahik War were distributed from the late 1540s under a grant system by which land was given to settlers who had borne the cost of their own passage to the colony. An initial grant of 100 acres was made to each settler who had paid his own way; the figure was later reduced to 50 acres as land was taken up.

The system cost the colonial government nothing, the land being granted rather than sold and the settler having paid for his own removal. It attracted the younger sons of merchant houses of Zong and members of dispersed ruling houses, for whom the colony offered land that could not be obtained at home, and it produced a rapid increase in the colony's population.

=== Economy ===

The colony's western frontier was wetter than the neighbouring colony of [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]], which had a drier climate and an agriculture suited to fruit such as oranges. Sinchew's climate, tropical monsoon and savanna with a pronounced dry season, supported crops of higher value: sugarcane, and tea in the higher ground. The colony's exports in the period were cash crops, timber and fish.

Labour for the plantations was obtained from two sources in this period: Zong settlers held under indenture, and the indigenous peoples of the region, who were taken captive in the course of the fighting and sold to plantation owners in the colony. The [[Secret Trade]] had not yet begun, and no people from [[Fosia]] were carried to Sinchew before the 1570s.

The enslavement of indigenous peoples was unlawful. The [[Zong Edict of 1500]] prohibited the enslavement of any person within all territories of the Kingdom of Zong, and it extended to the overseas colonies; it was not enforced there. The colonial government was complicit in the traffic, and the record indicates that the enslavement of the region's peoples was tolerated on the ground that those enslaved were not Zong.

=== General Assembly ===

The colony's landowners were given a forum in 1549, the year the war began, under the name of the General Assembly. It was not an elected body and held no power beyond that of advising the administration. Its principal concern was the shortage of land, and the demand for the lands of the western peoples was carried from the Assembly to the colonial government. The institution later influenced the [[General Assembly]] of Zong, established in the 17th century, which took its name from the colonial body.

=== Pressure on land ===

The grants produced demand for land that the colony did not possess. Land near the coast, within reach of the protection of [[Tamsui]], became difficult to obtain, and few settlers were willing to take land further inland, which was exposed to raiding.

Plantations were consequently built as enclosed and defensible estates, the walled plantation becoming the characteristic form of the Sinchew interior, and their owners hired armed men for protection. The clearing of forest for cane, tea and timber was extensive, and no account was taken of the claims of the peoples whose country it was.

== Prelude ==

The first encounters between the colony and the Salowi were maritime. The colony's fisheries began to operate in waters the Salowi regarded as their own, and the disputes that followed were the earliest armed engagements between the two. By the late 1540s they had become a standing condition of the western coast.

The Salowi raids on plantations near the frontier were a further and continuing cause of conflict, and it was these raids that the colony's mercenary forces were first engaged to answer.

The Salowi maintained an account of the destruction of the [[Kawesa]] chiefdoms by arriving peoples, and the record of the period indicates that they met the expansion of the colony with a hostility informed by that account.

== Western peoples ==

The Salowi occupied the westernmost territory of the region, a lobe of about 114,639 km² enclosed by water, with a coastline of about 2,001 km, the longest of any people of the region, and a median annual rainfall of about 2,563 mm. The Chidoha occupied the western interior toward the Gulf of Louyang, a territory of about 87,577 km², drier than the Salowi country and with the least rainfall of the peoples of the region. The two territories together comprise about 202,216 km².

Neither people was a state. Neither maintained a standing army, a central administration capable of directing a campaign, or a treasury. Their resistance was conducted by the communities of the affected districts, and there is no record of any authority able to conclude a surrender on behalf of either people as a whole.

== Forces ==

=== Sinchew Colony ===

The colony possessed no forces of its own sufficient to conduct a campaign on the western frontier and did not request troops from Zong. Its garrison was supplemented by hired mercenaries.

=== Sea Wolves ===

The principal force available to the colony was the [[Sea Wolves]], Sanese pirates and maritime mercenaries who had by the sixteenth century become trans-oceanic troops operating under no sovereign and were known to the Illypnian colonial powers as the Shendan Demons. They were already established as the backbone of the colonial garrison system. The force they supplied to the campaign numbered 450.

=== Hahkina ===

The [[Hachinawa|Hahkina state]], the colony's ally since the [[Treaty of Anping]] of 1538, supplied 25,000 warriors for the campaign in exchange for pork and iron. The Hahkina account records that they understood the campaign as a war and that they were supporting the Zong in it.

Both forces were deniable. The Sea Wolves were under no crown and the Hahkina were a sovereign ally acting on its own account, so that neither required the Zong government to have authorised their employment.

== Course of the war ==

The war lasted nine years, from 1549 to 1558. It was not fought as a series of battles between armies. The forces of the colony and its allies moved against settlements, destroying property, killing, and taking captives for sale to plantation owners in the colony.

The campaign was directed at communities rather than at an opposing army, and no narrative record of its course was kept. The dates of individual operations, the sequence of the advance, and the identity of the commanders are not recorded. What the record contains is the destruction and the displacement and the scale of the enslavement, on which the later characterisation of the conflict as a war rests.

== Zong's response ==

The campaign was conducted without the authorisation of the crown. Zong had no wish for a new war in the New World in the period: the Second Mahik War fell within the [[First Lurandian Native Wars]], whose cost in garrisons and supply across the [[Shendan Ocean]] consumed a growing share of the court's revenue, and returning settlers' accounts of colonial violence had begun to affect opinion on the mainland. It was fought in the last decade of the reign of [[Tiancheng of Zong|Emperor Tiancheng]], who reigned from 1521 and died in 1560.

The crown learned of the annexation. The [[Societal Assembly]] was informed of the expansion of the colony and raised no objection to it and required no alteration to it. The conquest had been made without troops from the mainland and had added to the territory and revenue of the crown at no cost to it.

The campaign was conducted throughout by the colony's second governor, [[Ông Kok-hong]], who had held the office since 1546. He was honoured for the acquisition of the western lands, and the honour stands in the record beside the absence of any contemporary description of the campaign as a war.

== Casualties ==

No record of the casualties of the Salowi and the Chidoha was kept, and no census existed against which their losses could be measured. The number of either people killed in the campaign is not recorded.

Disease accounted for a large part of the mortality. The campaign was accompanied by outbreaks among both peoples, carried into the country by the soldiers of the invading forces and spread between communities by people fleeing the fighting. The record does not distinguish deaths by disease from deaths by violence, and the losses of the two peoples are not separable into those causes.

The accounts of the campaign record that the forces engaged not infrequently came upon settlements that had been abandoned, or in which the dead remained unburied. Such villages are among the evidence on which the scale of the mortality among the Salowi and Chidoha rests, the fighting having been confined to a part of the country while disease and flight affected it more widely.

The losses of the colony's forces are recorded for the two contingents engaged. The [[Sea Wolves]] sustained 90 casualties out of a force of 450. The [[Hachinawa|Hahkina]] lost 2,620 killed from the 25,000 warriors they supplied. No figure is recorded for the enslaved, though the enslavement of the two peoples is recorded as having been on a scale sufficient to alter the population of the western lands.

=== Estimated losses ===

No count of the Salowi and Chidoha population was made before the war, and no enumeration of either people was attempted afterwards. The losses of the two peoples are therefore known only through estimation, and no figure was arrived at in the period itself.

Later estimates are constructed from the area of the two territories and from the population density of comparable societies of the region. The two territories together comprise about 203,192 km², and a density of about 0.24 persons per square kilometre has been adopted for both, the Salowi country being a coastal lobe with a long coastline and a high rainfall and the Chidoha country a drier interior, but neither supporting a denser population than the other by a margin the evidence can establish.

